## DEFINITION
PDB (Protein Data Bank) is a global database that stores the three-dimensional (3D) structures of proteins, nucleic acids, and other biological macromolecules.

## PURPOSE
- Store and share 3D macromolecular structures.
- Support structural biology research.
- Analyze protein structure and function.
- Aid in drug discovery and molecular modeling.

## COMMON USES
- Protein structure visualization.
- Structure-function analysis.
- Drug and ligand interaction studies.
- Comparative structural analysis.

## INFORMATION AVAILABLE
- 3D molecular structures.
- Protein and nucleic acid sequences.
- Experimental methods (X-ray, NMR, Cryo-EM).
- Ligand and functional annotations.

## IMPORTANT FILE FORMATS
- PDB (.pdb)
- mmCIF (.cif)
- PDBx/mmCIF (.cif)
- XML (.xml)

## FILES TO UPLOAD
- PDB (.pdb)
- mmCIF (.cif)
- Protein structure files.
- PDB ID (e.g., 1CRN).

## IMPORTANT POINTS
- World's primary repository for 3D biomolecular structures.
- Contains experimentally determined structures.
- Freely accessible for research and education.
- Widely used in structural biology and bioinformatics.
